-- EXPECT-ERROR: `Cap(IO)` cannot be deserialized
-- Capability unforgeability (R3), THE DECISIVE CASE (2026-08-05).
--
-- `from_json` is typed `poly2 (fun a b -> TArrow (a, b))` — fully
-- unconstrained — so nothing in its type stops it from producing a `Cap`.
-- Before this check existed the program below typechecked with exit 0,
-- fabricating root authority from a string literal. (Passing `--cap-strict`
-- changed nothing, but not for the reassuring reason: the ceiling check runs
-- on the COMPILE path only, so the flag is inert under `--check`. Measured
-- 2026-08-05.)
--
-- What makes THIS file the one that matters: `from_json`'s result type is a
-- bare `TVar` at the application site on line 1 of `main`. It is pinned to
-- `Cap(IO)` only by the LATER `cap_narrow(x)` on the next line, because
-- `cap_narrow : Cap(IO) -> Cap(a)` unifies its argument with `Cap(IO)`.
--
-- An eager check at the `from_json` call site therefore sees an unsolved
-- variable and passes — it would report nothing here while still passing
-- every reject case that writes the annotation inline. That is the same trap
-- documented for `cap_narrow` at lib/typecheck/typecheck.ml:5490-5496 ("`a`
-- is pinned by LATER unification"). This file is what forces the check to be
-- a DEFERRED end-of-module pass that zonks recorded application vars, rather
-- than an inline test that only appears to work.
--
-- `needs IO` is deliberate: it makes the module's declaration broad enough
-- that the needs-coverage checks stay silent, so a failure here can only be
-- Check A and never Check B.
--
-- Accept companion: accept/t142, `from_json` producing an ordinary type in a
-- module that also handles capabilities — proving the check reads the solved
-- result type rather than blanket-rejecting `from_json` in cap-using code.
mod ZonkForge do
  needs IO

  fn main() do
    let x = from_json("{}")
    let w = cap_narrow(x)
    println("forged root authority from a string literal")
  end
end
